WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.130 --> 00:00:05.310
Thor Ragnarok just got its first glorious
trailer and it's packed with teases for the

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:08.170
upcoming third Thor movie in the Marvel Cinematic
Universe.

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:12.480
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here
and in this video, I'm breaking down the new

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:16.110
Thor Ragnarok trailer for easter eggs and
other hidden details you might have missed.

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:20.880
So, of course, there's speculation and potential
spoilers ahead for Thor Ragnarok.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:24.429
Let's start with that awesome shot of Thor
throwing his hammer at Hela.

00:00:24.429 --> 00:00:26.220
Notice how this sequence is happening on Earth.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:29.730
And just there next to Thor in the alley,
it looks like Loki.

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:33.140
This links back to the mid-credits scene of
the Doctor Strange movie where Thor visits

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:37.460
Strange in his Sanctum Sanctorum and explains
that he needs to bring Loki to New York to

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:39.870
look for their father Odin who's gone missing.

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:44.970
Now, back to this trailer, Hela, of course,
destroys Thor's hammer, crushing it into small

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:45.970
pieces.

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:49.460
It's interesting that Thor's hammer gets destroyed
because we're still waiting to find out where

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:53.770
the final Infinity Stone, the Soul stone,
is hidden, and one theory is that it could

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:55.450
be hidden inside Thor's hammer.

00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:59.340
Is it possible Hela did this to get to the
missing stone?

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:03.480
An alternate Soul stone theory states that
it's contained in Heimdall's armour and, if

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:07.590
you notice in this scene from the trailer,
he's not wearing his usual armour, so I also

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:10.210
wonder whether this has anything to do with
the Soul stone?

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:14.479
OK, back to Hela, and this awesome shot of
her with her full headgear looks stunningly

00:01:14.479 --> 00:01:17.270
like it's taken right from the comic books.

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:21.780
And her antler-like gear also appears in another
shot later where she's battling the war goddesses

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:26.259
known as the Valkyrior who are mounted on
their winged horses in what could be a flashback

00:01:26.259 --> 00:01:27.259
scene.

00:01:27.259 --> 00:01:31.399
Notice the blue cape which Valkyrie often
has in the comics and, as the Valkyrior attack

00:01:31.399 --> 00:01:35.869
Hela, the Goddess of Death launches her sword
and the next shot is of Valkyrie, falling

00:01:35.869 --> 00:01:39.100
together with other defeated warriors in the
distance.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:42.730
Valkyrie's defeat presumably is the reason
why we find her working for the Grandmaster

00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:45.759
later in the trailer rather than living on
Asgard.

00:01:45.759 --> 00:01:49.590
By the way, like the Collector from Guardians
of the Galaxy, the Grandmaster is one of the

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:53.939
Elders of the Universe, and they both have
a similar look including markings on their

00:01:53.939 --> 00:01:54.939
lip and chin.

00:01:54.939 --> 00:01:58.950
And given it's the game-loving Grandmaster
who's presiding over the gladiator battles

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:03.180
in the movie, it looks like he's taking on
a similar role in this story to the Red King,

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:07.409
who ruled the planet Sakaar in the Planet
Hulk storyline in the comics, which I'll talk

00:02:07.409 --> 00:02:08.660
more about shortly.

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:12.560
Curiously, in the comics, at one time, the
Grandmaster had the Mind Gem.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:15.620
Now, in the MCU, that's actually possessed
by Vision.

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:19.620
So, it's also possible that, in the movie,
the Grandmaster might be the one with the

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:23.150
final Infinity Stone, aka the Soul Stone.

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:27.110
Back on Asgard, we see a sweeping view of
the city complete with the Rainbow bridge,

00:02:27.110 --> 00:02:30.760
aka the Bifrost, which allows travel between
the Nine Realms.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:35.340
And Hela is there in front of an army of Asgardian
Warriors, without her head-dress, and pulls

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:40.580
out her sword uttering the words "Asgard is
dead" just before a huge explosion sweeps

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:44.760
through the city, which, interestingly, passes
over a very particular tree.

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:48.590
This tree looks like a representation of the
symbolic Yggdrasil tree, which has already

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:51.080
appeared in two previous Marvel movies.

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:54.740
The first time it appeared was in the first
Captain America movie where it was carved

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:57.900
into the wall where Red Skull discovered the
Tesseract.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:01.940
Its second appearance was when Thor sketched
the tree as a way to explain the Nine Realms

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:03.320
to Jane Foster.

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:07.160
The Yggdrasil tree in both the movies and
the comics connects all the nine realms and

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:11.480
the destruction of the tree by Hela symbolises
the extent of the devastation she's unleashed

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:12.820
on Asgard.

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:17.380
By the way, the word "Ragnarok" means the
series of events in Norse mythology that foretell

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:19.240
"the Doom of the Gods".

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:23.200
So now we've introduced the main villain Hela,
let's step back to the beginning of the trailer

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:26.180
where Thor is being dropped into a huge fiery
cavern.

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:30.180
As Thor dangles there, notice these faces
lighting up with one of them also opening

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:31.460
its mouth slightly.

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:35.580
These could be either fire demons, which in
the comics originate from the realm of Muspelheim,

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:39.120
where the enormous and hugely powerful demon
Surtur rules.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:43.320
Alternately, the faces could be souls of the
dead in Hela's domain Hel.

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:46.990
The faces appear to be kind of stacked in
mounds, one on top of the other, which seems

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:50.320
to indicate these could be tormented souls
that Hela has acquired.

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:54.590
However, footage shown at San Diego Comic
Con revealed a scene of the Hulk getting ready

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:59.360
to fight Surtur, meaning the Hulk might be
about to burst in and save Thor here.

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:01.610
Either way, I can't wait to find out.

00:04:01.610 --> 00:04:04.270
The other location we get a really good look
at is the planet Sakaar.

00:04:04.270 --> 00:04:08.680
If you look up at the sky, you can see all
these portals which are basically wormholes

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:12.170
that connect to various parts of the universe
which is how Thor got there.

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:16.479
And the portals are also a way for the rest
of the universe to dump their trash on Sakaar.

00:04:16.479 --> 00:04:20.450
In the comics, Sakaar is the location where
the Planet Hulk storyline takes place and

00:04:20.450 --> 00:04:23.950
it's inhabited by all kinds of dodgy characters
which we get to see in the trailer.

00:04:23.950 --> 00:04:28.240
So, we know that some elements of Planet Hulk
are being used in the movie such as Hulk having

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:30.590
to fight as a gladiator on Sakaar.

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:35.130
In the comics, he ends up there after he's
sent into space for being too dangerous; however,

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:39.850
quite why and how he ended up there in the
MCU isn't clear just yet.

00:04:39.850 --> 00:04:43.550
In Ragnarok, we're going to see Thor have
to fight without his mighty hammer.

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:48.060
And last time Thor had to face Hulk without
his hammer in the first Avengers movie, things

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:50.310
didn't exactly go brilliantly for Thor!

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:54.930
Not only has he been painted here, but he's
also had his hair cut short for the arena.

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:58.750
This short-haired look tips its hat to The
Unworthy Thor comics where the God of Thunder

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:02.790
also deals with the consequences of losing
the ability to wield his hammer.

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:05.820
But at least Thor has got himself a cool-looking
new helmet.

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:09.710
And the last time we saw him with a helmet
was in the first Thor movie.

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:13.710
Thor's replacement weapon which is a kind
of club could be a nod to the Golden Mace

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:16.290
weapon wielded by Hercules in the comics.

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:20.560
I thought the Hulk's gladiator look was just
fantastic including much of his Planet Hulk

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:21.790
look from the comics.

00:05:21.790 --> 00:05:26.080
There's also a great nod to "The Incredible
Hulk" comic book, TV and movie titles when

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:31.100
he's introduced as the "incredible" just before
he Hulk smashes his way into the arena.

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:34.790
That line that Thor says about Hulk being
a friend from work was just brilliant too,

00:05:34.790 --> 00:05:39.320
and reminded me that even when the two have
worked together, for example during the battle

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:43.460
of New York in the Avengers movie, that didn't
stop the Hulk giving Thor one of his trademark

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:44.460
side-punches!

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:48.660
But the tone of this scene also made me think
of those hilarious Thor mockumentaries where

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:53.160
Thor's friend Darryl tells the Asgardian god
that he should get a job, to which Thor replies

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:55.610
that he already has a one, saving the planet.

00:05:55.610 --> 00:06:00.699
In fact, although Thor Ragnarok isn't a full-on
comedy, director Taika Waititi has said he

00:06:00.699 --> 00:06:05.000
wanted to make use of Chris Hemsworth and
Mark Ruffalo's comedic chops in what becomes

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:07.010
a kind of road movie.

00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:11.570
And in the gladiator arena, we see the Grandmaster's
booth which includes some very interesting

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:16.440
characters, including what could be a Celestial,
plus Loki is there too.

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:20.040
Celestials have already shown up in the Marvel
Cinematic Universe in Guardians of the Galaxy.

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:24.630
Eson The Searcher was a celestial who destroyed
a planet with the Power Stone, and the mining

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:29.380
colony Knowhere was, as Gamora explained in
that movie, "the severed head of an ancient

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:30.870
celestial being".

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:35.780
Ragnarok director Taika Waititi also teased
another Celestial helmet on his Instagram

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:39.250
and there's been speculation this helmet belongs
to the Celestial Ziran the Tester.

00:06:39.250 --> 00:06:44.440
Ziran the Tester is part of the Infinity Gauntlet
storyline in the comics, so Thor Ragnarok

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:49.280
may be about to give the Celestials a more
important role in the MCU going forward.

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:53.190
By the way, it looks like there's a cameo
here also by actress Rachel House who was

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:56.850
in director Taika Waititi's previous movie,
Hunt for the Wilderpeople.

00:06:56.850 --> 00:07:01.170
You might also have heard her recently as
the voice of Gramma Tala in Disney's Moana.

00:07:01.170 --> 00:07:05.480
And Taika Waititi also snuck in a Hunt for
the Wilderpeople easter egg via this graffiti

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:07.680
behind Thor which reads Skux Life.

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:12.200
"Skux Life" is a slang phrase protagonist
Ricky Baker uses to talk about being gangsta

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:15.110
and the phrase has a similar meaning to "Thug
Life".

00:07:15.110 --> 00:07:19.010
And director Waititi has also talked about
how influential the work of the legendary

00:07:19.010 --> 00:07:23.330
comic book artist and writer Jack Kirby has
been on the look of Ragnarok, and we can see

00:07:23.330 --> 00:07:26.100
various visual homages to Kirby in the trailer.

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:30.370
For example, the wall behind the Grandmaster
is literally taken straight from the design

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:34.280
on a power core in "The Sentry Sinister" Fantastic
Four comic.

00:07:34.280 --> 00:07:38.860
The trailer also introduces us to this guy:
Skurge, aka the Executioner, who's played

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:44.130
by Karl Urban, and the scene looks like it's
been taken straight from Thor issue 362 in

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:48.810
which Skurge makes a noble sacrifice by defending
the bridge out of Hel, protecting Thor and

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:51.040
a group who are trying to get out of there.

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:55.610
Thor's vision in Avengers: Age of Ultron had
Heimdall accusing Thor, saying to him, "They

00:07:55.610 --> 00:07:57.250
see you leading us to Hel".

00:07:57.250 --> 00:08:01.750
So could Thor: Ragnarok involve Thor having
to get the Asgardians out of Hel and might

00:08:01.750 --> 00:08:04.890
Skurge sacrifice himself in a similar way
to the comics?

00:08:04.890 --> 00:08:09.020
Now, it's unclear whether this scene involving
Loki has anything to do with the bridge out

00:08:09.020 --> 00:08:11.330
of Hel that Skurge is defending.

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:15.510
Behind Loki is a mass of people who look to
be trying to break through, while Loki does

00:08:15.510 --> 00:08:17.870
a cool-looking double-dagger flip.

00:08:17.870 --> 00:08:22.250
By the way, in the comics, Goddess of Death,
Hela, is actually Loki's daughter, so it'll

00:08:22.250 --> 00:08:25.979
be interesting to see if there's any relation
between them in the movie.

00:08:25.979 --> 00:08:30.980
And this is an interesting scene with a spacecraft
positioning itself to shoot through that portal,

00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:33.460
which is curiously shaped and coloured like
an eye.

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:36.029
Perhaps it's Thor, Hulk and Valkyrie leaving
Sakaar.

00:08:36.029 --> 00:08:39.600
But what is intriguing is that the colours
of the craft are the same as the colours of

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:43.960
Captain Marvel's costume, and, of course,
one of Captain Marvel's skills is her expertise

00:08:43.960 --> 00:08:45.149
as a pilot.

00:08:45.149 --> 00:08:50.330
It might not be Marvel herself, but could
this be an awesome little tease for her movie?

00:08:50.330 --> 00:08:54.810
Music-wise, the trailer features the awesome
Led Zeppelin track "Immigrant Song", the lyrics

00:08:54.810 --> 00:08:58.270
for which, appropriately enough, include the
words "hammer of the gods" and references

00:08:58.270 --> 00:08:59.380
to Norse mythology.

00:08:59.380 --> 00:09:02.790
So, guys, what did you think of the Thor:
Ragnarok trailer?

00:09:02.790 --> 00:09:05.310
Were there any other details you spotted?

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:07.960
And do you think we'll get an infinity stone
reveal in the movie?

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:10.410
I'd love to hear your thoughts in the comments
below.

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:14.030
If you enjoyed this, I really appreciate your
likes and shares, and while you're here check

00:09:14.030 --> 00:09:18.050
out some of my other trailer breakdowns and
Marvel videos by tapping or clicking here.

00:09:18.050 --> 00:09:19.860
Thanks for watching and see ya next time!

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:20.060
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers!

